    {
      "filename": "Cwm Badau-Bay of boats, Abercastle 1982 - geograph.org.uk - 912480.jpg",
      "title": "File:Cwm Badau-Bay of boats, Abercastle 1982 - geograph.org.uk - 912480.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Cwm_Badau-Bay_of_boats%2C_Abercastle_1982_-_geograph.org.uk_-_912480.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cwm_Badau-Bay_of_boats,_Abercastle_1982_-_geograph.org.uk_-_912480.jpg",
      "width": 640,
      "height": 491,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "ceridwen",
      "description": "Cwm Badau/Bay of boats, Abercastle 1982 First mentioned in 1566 as a safe harbour within this deeply indented notch which is actually a drowned valley. In the early C19 sloops based here were trading with Bristol and Liverpool and as late as the  1920s local oats and herrings were exported, and coal imported, by sea.\nAbercastle is named after the fortified rocky island Ynys y Castell that lies off the coast just to the north.",
      "dateCreated": "1982-07-21",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "abercastle-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gchr",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/c/h/r/abercastle-wp/cwm-badau-bay-of-boats-abercas-1-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Abercastle at low tide - geograph.org.uk - 932546.jpg",
      "title": "File:Abercastle at low tide - geograph.org.uk - 932546.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Abercastle_at_low_tide_-_geograph.org.uk_-_932546.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abercastle_at_low_tide_-_geograph.org.uk_-_932546.jpg",
      "width": 640,
      "height": 425,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Adrian Platt",
      "description": "Abercastle at low tide Potting boat high and dry in the harbour at Abercastle, with the tide on the way in. Local fishermen are somewhat restricted by the tides in this inlet, but the crabs they catch in their pots are delicious freshly cooked in the pub at nearby Mathry ! Local residents the oystercatcher and herring gull can also be seen looking for food !",
